This was a superb performance by Tottenham, who dominated possession and co=
ntrolled the game. They outplayed Chelsea and, yes, they had a touch of for=
tune that the home goalkeeper making his full league debut, Mike Penders, p=
unched the ball into his own net from a corner for the opening goal. But th=
ey kept their heads while their opponents combusted. How often has it been =
the inverse, that Tottenham would collapse, crumble or self-sabotage at key=
 junctures in the game?</p><p=20
style=3D"margin:0;font-size:18px;mso-line-height-alt:27px"><br>
There was a moment when that could have happened, too, early in the second =
half, when James Maddison hobbled off injured. Maddison, who made his first=
 start for 536 days and scored against Coventry City last week, was the chi=
ef conductor here. In the first half, he combined smartly with the new-mone=
y midfield of Mateus Fernandes and Sandro Tonali, creating overloads before=
 swinging the ball left to Mathys Tel, whose constant running at Josh Achea=
mpong would finally reap rewards in the second half, with a remarkable goal=
 after a 60-yard run.
